Bubbles,/users/527690f00000640001535cc4,GB,17.0,2024-05-30 17:29:58+00:00,1.0,Legal Cooling Off period beig ignored trying to complicate matter & not give refund,"I have a legal right to cancel in the 14 days cooling off period under distance selling regulations. I cancelled the £620 purchase of an O2 SIM from a 3rd party seller when I found it did not work in my area - contrary to what their website says - but the seller would not cancel. I cancelled on the advice of O2 where, after several attempts to check the SIM activation over the phone with O2 who could not find or access it, I took it into an O2 store who advised me to cancel it as that SIM and Mobile Broadband does not work where I live. I had checked the coverage checker before I bought it but was told that was the anticipated coverage after the upgrade was rolled out, their internal map told a different story. I cancelled it there and then while I was in store. My only option is a wired connection which I have now bought. I then contacted Amazon.co.uk 8 days ago under the A-Z Guarantee thinking this was a crystal clear case of cancelling in the cooling off period. I cancelled in the cooling off period in writing. It is in Amazon messages. What is there to argue about. Give me my refund. But no. I find that they say the decision is 7 days. THEN, if you add any details or chase because it is a simple case, THEY RESTART THE 7-DAY CLOCK. We are now on day 8 and as I chased/added proof, it will now be a minimum of 15 days until a decision and I cant chase before as it will restart the clock. Why will it take 15 days to 'decide' whether I can have my £620 back when the service does not work and I cancelled in the cooling off period. Even when I put in the claim over the phone, it was not actually filed the first time. I am not sure if this is deliberate to get rid of the semi-serious claimants but after I did not get an email confirming my £620 refund, I called again and found it had never been filed. i was told not to worry the second time, but then got a huge email requiring me to report this as a theft to the police and it went on and on. It seems the claim that was filed was filed incorrectly on my behalf saying i had not received it, then someone at Amazon took the agent's  filing as the version to work from and out of the blue introduced that looking at my messages about cancelling in the cooling off period and the agents filing, the item must have been stolen, and then proceeded to design a prolonged and convoluted way forward which involved reporting to the police and uploading the reports and it went on and on. This is just to delay the inevitable - whether I have to take Amazon or the seller to court as it was Amazon I found and used the seller from, and Amazon's A-Z guarantee that gives you false commitment to safety of your purchase. They are ACTUALLY trying to get the seller to keep my £620 for providing something that does not work and something I cancelled in the cooling off period in writing through their system so they can see. The A-Z Guarantee is not actually a guarantee that your money is safe. This is causing me so much stress. My mother has just died and I cant even communicated about her funeral service without getting dressed and driving for miles to get to an area I can use the internet. I sent them proof I have bought into a full fibre wired connection now as that is the only thing that will work. Still no response. I want to upload a no internet screenshot I took from home but funnily enough, I cant. Do not trust the A-Z Guarantee. Youl could not have a more straightforward case than mine. The service provider themselves told me to cancel as it would not work, I am in the cooling off period, and here we are 8 days later with no expected decision for another week. It is so stressful not to be able to chase or ask without the clock resetting. It is very manipulative and not at ALL cusomer 'service'.",2024-05-22 00:00:00+00:00,2024.0,5.0,30.0,3788,