Karl Nelson,/users/5c91063add5db25dd78a3329,GB,81.0,2023-08-25 20:11:59+00:00,1.0,Sick and tired of repeeatedly being tricked into signing up for Amazon Prime,"I am sick and tired of Amazon trying to trick me into signing up for Amazon Prime.  I often have to phone them, not easy, to get it cancelled.  Today, I clicked on the box to decline a free trial of Amazon prime.  The next screen said I was getting a 30 day free trial which I HAD ALREADY DECLINED.  There was no button to decline again.  I also got a similar box on the next screen meaning I had to phone Amazon to find out what was going on.  Why are Amazon like this?  When a customer declines an offer that should be enough.  I consider their practice deceptive and it's about time the authorities in this country put a stop.  Once Amazon prime has been declined it should never again appear on a subsequent screen during the placing of that particular order.  What a nasty, horrible company to deal with.  They are too big and too powerful and need cutting down to size.  I have also heard rumours that Amazon is withholding money from sellers.  If that is true then it is a thoroughly despicable act and, again, the authorities should be taking action.",2023-08-25 00:00:00+00:00,2023.0,8.0,25.0,1058,196,76,13,0.0,0,0,1,0,Genuine,sick tired amazon trying trick signing amazon prime often phone easy get cancelled today clicked box decline free trial amazon prime next screen said getting day free trial already declined button decline also got similar box next screen meaning phone amazon find going amazon like customer decline offer enough consider practice deceptive time authority country put stop amazon prime declined never appear subsequent screen placing particular order nasty horrible company deal big powerful need cutting size also heard rumour amazon withholding money seller true thoroughly despicable act authority taking action,0.05973909742087782,0.9402609025791222,0.10992708917554683,0.2,0.7921158691246704,79.2115