Anthony Baker,/users/584944ef0000ff000a5f6ebb,GB,32.0,2024-07-24 09:48:25+00:00,1.0,Wrong,"Amazon have attached reviews of Martin Scorsese's American film 'The Age of Innocence' to a different dvd altogether, Alan Bridges' Canadian film 'Age of Innocence'.  They are not even adaptations of the same source.  The Scorsese is from Edith Wharton's novel, whereas the Bridges is a screen original.  I    first told them of the error two years ago.  They refuse point blank to make a correction or to publish my review making it.  Indeed, they have banned me from reviewing the dvd at all. (Update.  Amazon now threaten to ban me from all reviewing, on the ground that I reviewed a seller.  That is a lie.  Their behaviour is despicable.  They should thank me, not bully me.  I have emailed Jeff Bezos, but of course that will be ignored.   Further update.  The rot is international.  American Amazon has Americans' reviews of the wrong film. Amazon France has reviews of the wrong film in French.  Amazon Italia has reviews of the wrong film in Italian.  Yet another update.  Amazon apologised and undertook to investigate.  It did not last. The next time I tried to log on Amazon decided they needed to be sure it was me, and sent a one-time code to my email address. That wasn't enough.  They supposedly sent another code to my mobile with a number ending in 01.  This mobile does not exist.  I have no mobile.   I have never had a mobile.   Heaven knows where this fictional number came from.   Year after year after year this happens, and over and over and over I tell Amazon what a nonsense it is.  But this time Amazon has inexplicably disabled my account and imposed intolerable procedures to revive it.  I am ninety-one years old.  I cannot take this stress.   Another update.   Madness follows madness.  To confirm my identity, Amazon wants me to use the scanner I do not have to send a copy of the driving licence or the passport I do not have.  Talk about piling Pelion on Ossa.                                               Or are the most recent developments the work of a scammer impersonating Amazon, trying to persuade me to use the scanner I do not have to provide a copy of the passport I do not have? Later still.  Can I believe Amazon, who now say they have reinstated my account?                                     Yet later, they have - and apologised and credited me with £50 for my trouble.   I suppose I should give  them a second star.                                                      But now I find that - two or three brief recent ones excepted - Amazon has deleted all my past reviews, including a lengthy analysis of Francois Ozon's film 'L'Amant Double'.  I take this to have been punishment for the false accusation about a seller.   Taxed with this, Amazon have the brass neck to tell me to recreate my reviews.  I kept no copies and I have better things to do.  I have taken the second star away...                                                 Possible final update: the reviews of the wrong film have, at last, been removed.)                                                                                                                                                                      ",2022-02-23 00:00:00+00:00,2024.0,7.0,24.0,3140,498,5,1,882.0,0,0,0,0,Genuine,amazon attached review martin scorsese american film age innocence different dvd altogether alan bridge canadian film age innocence even adaptation source scorsese edith wharton novel whereas bridge screen original first told error two year ago refuse point blank make correction publish review making indeed banned reviewing dvd update amazon threaten ban reviewing ground reviewed seller lie behaviour despicable thank bully emailed jeff bezos course ignored update rot international american amazon american review wrong film amazon france review wrong film french amazon italia review wrong film italian yet another update amazon apologised undertook investigate last next time tried log amazon decided needed sure sent onetime code email address wasnt enough supposedly sent another code mobile number ending mobile exist mobile never mobile heaven know fictional number came year year year happens tell amazon nonsense time amazon inexplicably disabled account imposed intolerable procedure revive ninetyone year old cannot take stress another update madness follows madness confirm identity amazon want use scanner send copy driving licence passport talk piling pelion os recent development work scammer impersonating amazon trying persuade use scanner provide copy passport later still believe amazon say reinstated account yet later apologised credited £ trouble suppose give second star find two three brief recent one excepted amazon deleted past review including lengthy analysis francois ozons film lamant double take punishment false accusation seller taxed amazon brass neck tell recreate review kept copy better thing taken second star away possible final update review wrong film last removed,5.377664592426979e-07,0.9999994622335407,0.27930454290521595,0.2,0.8558606396978136,85.58606396978135,High Trust
