Bstuff,/users/600dd2a749f28300192911e8,US,1.0,2021-01-24 20:04:28+00:00,1.0,Amazon’s handling of fraudulent gift card orders,"On Sunday, January 13th there were several orders for gift card placed on my Amazon Prime account by an unauthorized party who accessed my Amazon account between 7 and 8 AM. By pure serendipity, I accessed my account at about 8:00 that morning and, seeing the fraudulent orders, ended up reporting them to customer service within less than an hour of their being placed.  I believe the orders were placed around 7:45 and I called around 8:00.  I reported that neither I nor anyone with legitimate access to my account placed these orders.  They are, and were reported to Amazon as, fraudulent orders.When reported, Amazon's customer service representative told me they would take actions to invalidate these orders and recommended that I notify my credit card company that the orders were fraudulent.I did as recommended, however, roughly 10 days later Amazon locked my account and began sending me requests for an alternate credit card to which they could charge the gift cards.Amazon was notified of the fraudulent nature of these orders practically immediately that they happened and, I was told, intended to take appropriate actions to deal with them.  Based on their locking my account and their attempts to collect from my credit card company, I can assume that these actions were not taken.  I have spoken with Customer Service several times since this all began and, each time, have been assured that the issue would be resolved but every time, within a short time I have received another email either notifying me that Amazon was “currently working with your card issuer to resolve this dispute”, or requesting that I identify an alternate card to which the orders could be charged.Interestingly, while Customer Service seems sympathetic and helpful the responses I receive via email are from “Account Specialist, Amazon.com | Amazon Disputes Team”, are rarely signed by anyone, and seem to be worded the same depending on which of the forms I receive. Additionally, they never seem to respond directly to the emails I send in response.I've been an Amazon Prime Customer for something like 25 or 30 years (since the mid ‘90s) and have placed tens, if not hundreds, of thousands of dollars in orders over that period of time (I also use this account for purchases for my business) and yet, now, they are treating me as if I'm trying to cheat them out of a couple of hundred bucks?  Is this really how they should treat a customer who has been with them as long as I have and done as much business with them as I have?  I was taken completely by surprise, I really wouldn't have expected it of them.By the way, yesterday I discovered that they have also locked my Audible account even though, ostensibly, this is a different company. In the same vain as above I’ve been an Audible account holder for about 20 years and have something like 850 audible books in my library.  Several times I have asked Amazon to “Please” reopen my account and deal with these fraudulent orders as originally promised.  Thus far, no luck and, actually, no real responsive replies from the “Amazon Disputes Team”.",2021-01-24 00:00:00+00:00,2021.0,1.0,24.0,3099,527,48,7,0.0,0,0,1,0,Genuine,sunday january th several order gift card placed amazon prime account unauthorized party accessed amazon account pure serendipity accessed account morning seeing fraudulent order ended reporting customer service within less hour placed believe order placed around called around reported neither anyone legitimate access account placed order reported amazon fraudulent orderswhen reported amazon customer service representative told would take action invalidate order recommended notify credit card company order fraudulenti recommended however roughly day later amazon locked account began sending request alternate credit card could charge gift cardsamazon notified fraudulent nature order practically immediately happened told intended take appropriate action deal based locking account attempt collect credit card company assume action taken spoken customer service several time since began time assured issue would resolved every time within short time received another email either notifying amazon “currently working card issuer resolve dispute” requesting identify alternate card order could chargedinterestingly customer service seems sympathetic helpful response receive via email “account specialist amazoncom amazon dispute team” rarely signed anyone seem worded depending form receive additionally never seem respond directly email send responseive amazon prime customer something like year since mid ‘s placed ten hundred thousand dollar order period time also use account purchase business yet treating im trying cheat couple hundred buck really treat customer long done much business taken completely surprise really wouldnt expected themby way yesterday discovered also locked audible account even though ostensibly different company vain i’ve audible account holder year something like audible book library several time asked amazon “please” reopen account deal fraudulent order originally promised thus far luck actually real responsive reply “amazon dispute team”,0.0015753691010608204,0.9984246308989392,0.2955692652832305,0.2,0.8583261685061156,85.83261685061157,High Trust
